211: roots have length 2/m, where m = 1 if g is simply laced, i.e. belongs to the
212: series A, D or E , m = 2 if g belongs to the series B or C or is of type F4 , and
213: m = 3 if g is of type G2 . In the following, the symbol m will be reserved for
214: this number. For x ∈ h∗R \ {0}, we let x∨ = 2x/hx, xi. If Π = {α1 , α2 , . . . , αl }
215: is an arbitrary set of simple (basis) roots and if {λ1 , λ2 , . . . , λl } ⊆ h∗R is the set
216: of fundamental dominant weights relative to Π, i.e. hλi , α∨           j i = δij , then X is
217: the Z–lattice generated by {λ1 , λ2 , . . . , λl } and Y is the Z–lattice generated
218: by Π. Let ∆ be the set of roots. Then { α∨ | α ∈ ∆ } are the so-called dual
219: roots or coroots (relative to our inner product h·, ·i). (So in this paper a coroot
220: is in h∗R and not in h.) The coroot lattice Y ∨ is the Z–lattice generated by
221: {α∨             ∨
